#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120666} :activity/id #uuid "716a7135-4378-53ee-a90e-dd04ad76a6dc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049814 :topic/id #uuid "15c8115d-e746-5dd5-ab8a-ad335bac2d26" :topic/math-academy-id 1849} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121032 :tutorial/content "The **real coordinate space** (of dimension $n$) is the set of all possible column vectors of height $n,$ whose components are real numbers. Symbolically, we represent the real coordinate space of dimension $n$ as $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the following vectors are in $\\mathbb{R}, \\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^5,$ respectively: \n\n![](images/20/20e0adb04d7a8711cad4a9ee7ad26755d52176fc9549d7809fe10665a8118945.svg)\n\nWe've already encountered several operations on vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ or $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ namely, vector addition and subtraction, scalar multiplication, and the dot product. All of these operations work the same way in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "084d7eb8-e6bd-5678-98b5-149f18e61a8b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3182 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7383fe70-62a2-5560-94ff-c67e603fd1a9" :step/math-academy-id 17446 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119801 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119612 :question/id #uuid "13fa2a8c-de7c-59ee-a838-2997494edc56" :question/math-academy-id "e-4782"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ce19559-0f08-5ef7-9ce4-58e8e14094ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049537}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411272}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Multiple of a Vector"} :step/id #uuid "5cfee280-6899-59d1-9ef8-d9880087735a" :step/math-academy-id 17447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119802 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119613 :question/id #uuid "1e82713d-4063-5bcd-bc2e-1d35c831ab34" :question/math-academy-id "e-4783"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab0d2c2f-e7a4-51bb-a20a-f28c2f7ded8b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490284}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Multiples of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "5aec48fe-0c24-5fa5-ac63-78e2260f7ead" :step/math-academy-id 17448 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121033 :tutorial/content "In general, if we have two vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ and a scalar $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R},$ then our vector operations are defined as follows:\n\n- The **sum/difference** of the two vectors is defined as\n![](images/fc/fc78b2f4e9262108a217e57fec67e096b8b8b18e1486bec8e51df60b8afdfdfa.svg)\n\n- **Scalar multiplication** of a vector is defined as\n![](images/e7/e78025f3481423365b2e924b6f8ba4e59f41e0c644ced7d2263238b219cba27a.svg)\n\n- The **dot product** of two vectors is defined as\n\n$$\n\\quad \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = \\underbrace{u_1 \\cdot v_1 + u_2 \\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n \\cdot v_n}_{\\Large{n} \\,\\textrm{-terms}} = \\sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a465d6d9-270d-5cd3-98c8-a78c527b3558" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3183 :tutorial/title "General Rules"} :step/id #uuid "21085223-43d0-5726-b75d-b102bd093330" :step/math-academy-id 17449 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119614 :question/id #uuid "ba38ff28-b4f3-59b8-b18a-a278db26294d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4784"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec386a91-fc52-56d8-8531-7e78a8464380" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411268}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dot Product of Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "4acc22bf-58df-50bc-b180-3077f975037c" :step/math-academy-id 17450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119804 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119615 :question/id #uuid "3ec5f9d5-327b-59e5-8df7-fb37d4054021" :question/math-academy-id "e-4785"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94f8e0e5-8d8a-516a-8f46-d76e94305df4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368723}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Unknown Quantity in an Expression Involving the Dot Product"} :step/id #uuid "36416450-4cfa-51cd-be65-4dead60ba52e" :step/math-academy-id 17451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121062 :tutorial/content "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4,$ given by\n\n![](images/1f/1f1b79f2e61fccbb27944284cbdd217a311a7f795100cfbb56340a457814b407.svg)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b},$ all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5+(-5) \\\\ 1+3 \\\\ -3+1 \\\\ 6+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n![](images/6c/6c377966aa9fa1d581c0f9e3ba16bceb4020471914d5c4fb61118ff7cd709466.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "34cc8b84-ed7a-54e2-995c-12a2b57b0ff0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3922 :tutorial/title "Adding Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "6574b7ab-c36d-5af6-9074-943a1db9174c" :step/math-academy-id 23156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120668}}] :activity/title "Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120043}]}
